- Ran into an issue where multiple circles with different radii were generated on the same broccoli in an image,
so I added a fix where same-center detections are collapsed before Non Maximum Suppression (NMS).
This way, if the model predicts multiple circles around the same broccoli, only the one with the highest confidence will be kept for evaluation and visualization.

19/5
get_circle_iou_scalar (line 56) — pure-Python circle IoU using the three-case formula (no overlap / containment / partial). 
Mirrors circle_ops.py but works scalar for the mAP matching loop.

compute_circle_map (after compute_map) — a drop-in replacement for compute_map that takes (cx, cy, r) lists instead of (x1, y1, x2, y2) lists, 
and uses get_circle_iou_scalar for TP/FP matching. No "difficult" concept needed since your broccoli dataset doesn't use it.

convert_yolo_pred_circles (after convert_yolo_pred_x1y1x2y2) — returns predicted circles as (cx, cy, r) normalized 0–1, without converting to bounding squares. 
This is the correct format for circle IoU matching.

collate_function — now passes circles (the (cx, cy, r) GT tensor from the dataset) through to evaluate_map.

evaluate_map — branches on use_circles:
  Circle path: gets GT circles from batch['circles'], predicts with convert_yolo_pred_circles, converts to bounding squares only for NMS/dedup (which expect boxes), then uses actual circles for compute_circle_map.
  Box path: the original logic, unchanged.

The mAP number you get in circle mode will now be higher than before for a well-trained model, because a tight circle prediction is correctly rewarded —
a predicted circle that perfectly fits the broccoli head would have been penalized slightly by box IoU (background corners of the bounding square counted against it).



